<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pskov</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pskov is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s oldest cities, on the Velikaya river near the Estonian border, first recorded in the chronicles under 903. A trading town of the Novgorod lands, it became a self-governing republic with its own assembly by the fourteenth century and was a frontier fortress against the Livonian Order and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Vasily III annexed it to Muscovy in 1510.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pskov.jpg" alt="Pskov" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Олег Токарев · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%B8_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pskov stands on the Velikaya River in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 20 kilometres east of the Estonian border. Its name was originally Pleskov, which may be loosely translated as the town of purling waters. The earliest mention, under 903, records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-of-kiev">Igor of Kiev</a> married a local lady, Olga, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olga-of-kiev">Saint Olga</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. Pskovians sometimes take that year as the city's foundation, and in 2003 they marked its 1,100th anniversary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first prince of Pskov was Sudislav, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>. In the 12th and 13th centuries the town adhered politically to the Novgorod Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> took it in 1241, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> recaptured it several months later. To secure their independence from the knights the Pskovians elected a Lithuanian prince, Daumantas, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daumantas-of-pskov">Dovmont</a>, as their leader in 1266. He fortified the town, and the core of the citadel still bears the name Dovmont's town.</p><p>Novgorod formally recognised Pskov's independence in 1348, and it became the capital of the Pskov Republic. Its most powerful force was the merchants who traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. The veche promulgated a law code, the Pskov Charter, which was one of the principal sources of the all-Russian law code of 1497. The Kremlin withstood twenty-six sieges in the 15th century alone, and a local school of icon-painting flourished.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> annexed the city in 1510. Three hundred families were deported to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and merchants and military families from Muscovy were settled there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> it withstood a prolonged siege in 1581 and 1582 by a Polish-Lithuanian army of 50,000 under King Stephen Báthory, defended mainly by civilians. It also withstood a Swedish siege in 1615. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> ended its traditional role as a border fortress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pskov is the administrative centre of Pskov Oblast and preserves much of its medieval walls, built from the 13th century onwards. Within the Krom, the medieval citadel, stands Trinity Cathedral, founded in 1138 and rebuilt in the 1690s, which holds the tombs of the saint princes Vsevolod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daumantas-of-pskov">Dovmont</a>. The Churches of the Pskov School of Architecture were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pskov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its princes 'gathered the Russian lands' by purchase, inheritance, marriage, diplomacy and war, absorbing Nizhny Novgorod in 1392, Yaroslavl in 1463, Rostov in 1474, the Novgorod Republic in 1478, Tver in 1485, Vyatka in 1489, <b>Pskov</b> in 1510 and Ryazan around 1521.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The distant territories, including <b>Pskov</b> until it became independent by the Treaty of Bolotovo, traditionally dated to 1348, were governed by officials sent from the city or ruled through tribute, and the rural lands around the city were later organised into five districts, the pyatiny.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants bought furs, wax, flax and hides from Novgorod and <b>Pskov</b>, often through the Hanseatic counting house at Novgorod, the Peterhof, and shipped them west to Lübeck and Flanders, while salt, cloth, herring, wine and metals moved east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tsardom of Russia grew out of the Grand Duchy of Moscow, which under Ivan III (r. 1462–1505) and Vasily III (r. 1505–1533) had absorbed Novgorod, Tver, <b>Pskov</b> and Ryazan, thrown off the overlordship of the Great Horde and become the dominant power among the Russian principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries the smerds lost more of their freedom: in Novgorod and <b>Pskov</b> in the 14th and 15th centuries they were forbidden to seek a new master.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Rakvere in northern Estonia, an army of Novgorod and <b>Pskov</b> under Prince Dmitry of Pereslavl fought the forces of Danish Estonia and the Livonian Order.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-18">18 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
